"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[76651],{76651:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"content":"\\n## Setting up Swap File on Linux\\nWhen the system\'s <mark>physical RAM</mark> is insufficient, you can improve system stability by setting up a `Swap File` that uses a portion of the disk space as memory.\\n### Check Current Swap Status\\nFirst, check if there is any swap space already configured on the system.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Check current swap\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ swapon --show\\n# or\\n$ free -h\\n```\\n### Create Swap File\\nAllocate disk space to create a swap file.  \\nThis is an example of creating a <mark>2GB</mark> file.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Allocate swap file\\"\\n}\\n---\\n# Allocating 2048MB (2GB) with the name /swapfile\\n$ sudo dd if=/dev/zero of=/swapfile bs=1M count=2048\\n```\\n### Set Permissions\\nFor security, modify permissions so that only the <mark>root user</mark> can access the swap file.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Secure swap file\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ sudo chmod 600 /swapfile\\n```\\n### Format and Enable Swap\\nFormat the file as swap space and activate it on the system.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Activate swap\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ sudo mkswap /swapfile\\n$ sudo swapon /swapfile\\nNAME      TYPE SIZE USED PRIO\\n//[!code word:/swapfile]\\n/swapfile file   2G   0B   -2\\n```\\n### Permanent Configuration\\nRegister the swap setting in the `/etc/fstab` file so that it persists after reboot.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Add to fstab\\"\\n}\\n---\\n$ sudo vi /etc/fstab\\n// [!code ++]\\n/swapfile none swap sw 0 0\\n```\\n### Optimize Swappiness\\n<mark>Swappiness</mark> is a value (0-100) that determines how aggressively the system will use swap.  \\nFor desktops or servers, a low value around `10` is usually recommended.\\n```sh\\n{\\n    \\"title\\": \\"Adjust Swappiness\\"\\n}\\n---\\n# Check current value\\n$ cat /proc/sys/vm/swappiness\\n\\n# Temporary change (set to 10)\\n$ sudo sysctl vm.swappiness=10\\n\\n# Permanent reflection\\n$ sudo vi /etc/sysctl.conf\\n// [!code ++]\\nvm.swappiness=10\\n```\\n> If you are using an SSD, excessive swap writes can affect its lifespan, so unless memory is extremely low, it is recommended to use the <a href=\\"https://jooojub.github.io/snippets/content/2026-02-10-stash-how-to-configure-ZRAM-on-linux\\" target=\\"_blank\\">ZRAM</a> settings discussed earlier."}')}}]);